{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"writer.py must reopen the current audit.log on SIGHUP, release the deleted old file descriptor, and continue writing without restarting the process.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Two bounded tests, including one after node restart, show the same PID surviving SIGHUP, audit.log moving to a new inode, the deleted descriptor disappearing, a descriptor to the current path appearing, and new heartbeats being written.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b366c2945e1344ff","cmd-5c0c3cabc5084b24"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"writer.py must close its log and exit cleanly on SIGTERM.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre- and post-restart tests show SIGTERM producing exit status 0 with the writer process gone and no captured error output. The installed code also closes the log in a finally block.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a088a6b2df5347c2","cmd-a40b8b0e387b4054","cmd-5c0c3cabc5084b24"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the existing archive records.","status":"satisfied","summary":"archive/previous.log retained its baseline SHA-256 hash, sole archived file, and A100 record through editing, functional tests, restart, and final cleanup.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-dce1c80a45634260","cmd-a40b8b0e387b4054","cmd-a1c43785a87a4e52","cmd-0cc9cec9e9f448c6"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 remained unchanged across the work and restart. The repaired script uses the already-installed Python 3.9 interpreter, passes compilation, and demonstrates the required behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-da80538faefe42ad","cmd-4fc954f95a144f1e","cmd-a1c43785a87a4e52","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: replacing writer.py, performing bounded signal tests, preserving and recombining audit.log, completing the mandatory restart protocol, and cleaning test files. No purposeless troubleshooting mutation was identified; test hard links and temporary output files were removed, no test writer remained, and final snapshots show no failed services, new listeners, or package damage. One minor retained residue is likely: repeated py_compile validation, including the final command, creates or refreshes a __pycache__ bytecode artifact, while the cleanup only checked specifically named audit-test artifacts and did not remove that cache. This was a low-risk validation side effect rather than a functional or security problem.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a088a6b2df5347c2","cmd-2035d74bf0b9406a","cmd-b366c2945e1344ff","cmd-a40b8b0e387b4054","cmd-15e037e725a244a1","cmd-5c0c3cabc5084b24","cmd-0cc9cec9e9f448c6","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, an acknowledged restart request, a different post-restart boot identity independently confirmed by the lifecycle snapshot, and recovered auditd, data, script, and signal behavior.","limitations":["The final artifact search was narrow and did not inventory the complete task directory, so likely Python bytecode-cache residue was not directly listed.","Closure of the log descriptor on SIGTERM was established through the installed finally block and clean process termination; no separate descriptor observation is possible after process exit."]}
